[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: leaf_router_0_1
diagnosis_text:
The outage is not a host NIC/cabling issue. Both host links are up: pc_0_1:eth0 reports Link detected: yes and leaf_router_0_1:eth2 also shows link up. The symptom seen from both ends is “Destination Net Unreachable” returned by their default gateways (10.0.0.1 and 10.0.1.1), which indicates missing routing, not a layer-1 failure.

Root cause is that FRR/BGP is down on leaf_router_0_1. Evidence: systemctl on leaf_router_0_1 shows frr.service Active: inactive (dead), and vtysh returns bgpd is not running. Consequently, leaf_router_0_1 is not advertising 10.0.1.0/24 to the fabric; its own FRR routing table contains only connected routes (no BGP-learned prefixes). Upstream devices corroborate this: both spines and the super-spine have BGP routes for 10.0.0.0/24 but none for 10.0.1.0/24, and spine BGP to leaf_router_0_1 sits in Active state. Thus pc_0_0 traffic hits leaf_router_0_0, which has no route to 10.0.1.0/24 and generates Net Unreachable; the reverse path fails similarly at leaf_router_0_1.

Affected component: FRRouting (bgpd) daemon. Mechanism: lack of BGP advertisement for 10.0.1.0/24 removes end-to-end reachability. Localization: leaf_router_0_1 control plane (FRR service), host-facing eth2 link is healthy. Root cause: FRR service stopped on leaf_router_0_1. Corrective fix: restart FRR on leaf_router_0_1 (systemctl restart frr) and verify BGP neighbors to both spines come Up and that 10.0.1.0/24 is present on spines/super-spine and 10.0.0.0/24 appears on leaf_router_0_1. === END ===